content.thinking:
The JS is correct. mateLeft is 450px. So the calc is resolving to 450px.
The issue is that `--rail-left` is not being set on `[data-stage]` when the left rail is open, so the `calc(50vw - var(--rail-left))` resolves to just `50vw` (600 px on a 1200 px viewport).  

That leaves the character and input at 600 px from the left edge of the stage (which is now only 900 px wide), so the visual center is at 900 px instead of 600 px. The test expects the center to be within 1 px of the window center (600 px), hence the failure.

The root cause is almost certainly a selector mismatch: the CSS rule that sets `--rail-left` on `[data-stage]` when the left rail is open is not matching, or the rule is being overridden by a later rule, or the variable is being reset somewhere in the transition.

To fix it quickly, switch to the percentage-based formula you derived:

``` css
left: calc(50% + (var(--rail-right) - var(--rail-left)) / 2);
```

This uses percentages of the stage width (so it’s immune to any weird `vw` resolution) and automatically centers the character/input regardless of how many rails are open. You’ll also need `--rail-left` and `--rail-right` to actually be set on `[data-stage]` (or inherited from a parent that has them).

To make sure the variables are applied reliably, set them on a parent that definitely exists and use `:has()`:

``` css
[data-home-shell] {
  --rail-left: 0px;
  --rail-right: 0px;
}

[data-home-shell]:has(> [data-rail="left"]:not(:empty)) {
  --rail-left: var(--rail-width);
}

[data-home-shell]:has(> [data-rail="right"]:not(:empty)) {
  -- rail-right: var(--rail-width);
}
```

Then in `App.tsx` (or wherever the flex container lives):

```tsx
<div data-home-shell style={{ position: "absolute", inset: 0, display: "flex" }}>
  {home && <Rail left />}
  <div data-stage containerType /* ... rest of stage ... */>
    {home && <HomeScreen />}
    {settings}
  </div>
  {home && <Rail right />}
</div>
```

And update the `HomeScreen`/`HomeMate` positioning to use the new formula (or move the character/input into an overlay sibling of the flex row so `left: 50%` is truly 50 % of the window).

If you want to avoid any CSS variable headaches, the overlay approach you sketched (move character/input into a full-window absolute sibling) is also very reliable and avoids the variable issue entirely.

Try the `:has()` + percentage 50 % formula first — it should get the test passing immediately.

